  "source": {
    "title": "Lisburn Standard, 8 May 1936, p. 5 (\"Three new members joining the Urban Council: only eleven put forward\")",
    "publisher": "Lisburn Standard (via the British Newspaper Archive)",
    "kind": "news",
    "check": "Fourteen of the fifteen seats filled without a poll; the Central Ward vacancy is flagged. The printed middle name of S. D. B. Hanna is unclear on the image, so the initials used in the 1939 and 1946 reports are given.",
    "url": "https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0002913/19360508/072/0005"
  },
  "note": "Municipal elections, 15 May 1936. Only eleven names were put forward for fifteen seats, so there was no poll; the nominated candidates were returned unopposed and retiring members were declared deemed re-elected, leaving one Central Ward vacancy (Lisburn Standard, 8 May 1936, p. 5). Five nominations for six seats. 'As there was no retiring member the vacancy could not be filled by the returning officer under the regulations now in force', so one seat remained vacant. Returned unopposed."
